 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot W \cdot H)$, as every Gaussian is evaluated at every pixel. The matrix operations (inverse, multiplication) are constant time per Gaussian-pixel pair but dominate the constant factor.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot W \cdot H)$ for the intermediate `power` and `alphas` tensors. This is significantly higher than sparse approaches.
-- **Performance:** Highly parallelizable on GPUs due to the batched nature. Performance scales linearly with the number of Gaussians and pixels.
-- **Strengths:** Simple implementation, high throughput on modern GPUs, easy to integrate with automatic differentiation frameworks.
-- **Limitations:** High memory consumption for large scenes or high resolutions. Does not exploit spatial coherence or sparsity.
-- **When to use:** When the number of Gaussians and image resolution are moderate, and simplicity of implementation is prioritized. Not suitable for very large-scale scenes without further optimization (e.g., tiling, sparse representations).
